WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.130
Speaker 1: Today.

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:05.500
It's great to be joined by Democratic Congressman
Adam Schiff, who is a progressive leader in

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.309
Congress representing California's 30th District.

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:14.679
He is running for Senate, hoping to build
an economy that works for everyone and safeguard

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:19.180
our democracy, protect the planet, things
which really should not be controversial at

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.760
this point in time.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.720
Congressman, great to have you on.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.740
Listen, let me start with what I know is on
the forefront of many of my viewers minds,

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.130
which is, is anybody getting indicted at any
point?

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.790
You know, we've been going through this thing
for years and we've been talking about the

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.200
former president and associates and others.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.500
Where are you on this issue at this point
of what you expect to see?

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.610
Speaker 2: Yeah, you know, I have been fairly
conservative in my expectations, mainly because

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:51.260
I don't want to get my hopes up, but also
because the Justice Department has been so

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.090
slow to move for so long.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.930
They moved with alacrity when it came to the
people breaking into the Capitol on January

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.350
six and assaulting police officers.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:06.380
But it took them a very long time to investigate
the multiple efforts, multiple lines of effort

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.020
to overturn the election.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:14.150
The president's call with the Georgia secretary
of state, for example, but also his role in

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:19.940
the incitement of that attack, the higher
ups in that plot that culminated on January

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:20.940
6th.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:27.070
You know, we were way out ahead of them in
the investigation, which is very unusual for

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.510
Congress, as cumbersome as it is to be ahead
of the Justice Department.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:36.580
Yeah, I do think the appointment of a special
counsel was a positive sign because he seems

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:42.430
to be, you know, not not cautious like the
attorney general and leaning into the investigation

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:43.430
finally.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:50.000
I also am very impressed with what we see
out of Fulton County, Georgia.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.290
In many respects, I'm more optimistic about
the prosecutor, Fulton County, than I am about

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.820
the Justice Department.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:02.890
But I think prudently we should expect that
there's no prosecutor who will rescue the

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:08.500
country from this danger that we may just
very well have to defeat it at the polls again.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:14.240
But I will be happily proven wrong by either
prosecutor or the Manhattan D.A., which I

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:20.670
think is probably the the least likely candidate
to move forward, but nonetheless, more exposure

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:21.670
of the president.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:28.140
Speaker 1: So over the weekend, at a particularly
deranged edition of CPAC, Donald Trump held

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.440
sort of like an impromptu press conference
and was asked some version of the question,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.019
If you are indicted, however many times, will
that interfere with your presidential run?

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:38.840
And he said no.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:44.000
And the entire atmosphere at CPAC, I don't
know how much you saw, but one of the trends

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.060
is that the biggest applause lines are either
homophobic or more recently transphobic stuff.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.620
This really seems to get the modern right
excited.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.930
Maybe part of it is because there's a sort
of lack of policy outside of this sort of

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:58.930
rhetoric.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:04.010
How concerned are you about the degree to
which the transgender community now seems

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.569
to be the primary target of the MAGA movement?

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:09.909
Speaker 2: I'm deeply concerned about it.

00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:12.099
I'm a vice chair of the Equality Caucus.

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:18.010
So this is an issue I've been very focused
on, on behalf of my constituents.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:23.629
And you're right, just a couple of weeks ago,
Donald Trump did a video from Mar a Lago.

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:28.939
The whole purpose of which was to demonize
the trans community, basically to, I guess,

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:35.890
show his GOP Trump MAGA base that he's even
worse on those issues than Ron DeSantis.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:42.629
And this is the kind of primary that's going
on in the GOP, which is who can most dehumanize

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:47.650
one of the most vulnerable populations in
the country, one that has a very high, tragic

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:53.319
and very high suicide rate, as well as being
the victim of a lot of violence directed at

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:55.230
the community by others.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.760
So I'm deeply concerned about it.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.430
This is what autocrats and would be autocrats
do.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:05.590
They look to the most vulnerable and try to
dehumanize them.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:11.739
And it also, I think, is a signal of, you
know, other rights they want to take away

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:17.729
from Americans they've taken away or tried
to reproductive freedom through this multi-decade

00:04:17.729 --> 00:04:19.851
effort at the Supreme Court.

00:04:19.851 --> 00:04:24.130
And they may target other rights and freedoms
of other Americans, particularly vulnerable

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.130
ones.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.930
Speaker 1: You know, Senator Bernie Sanders
recently in an interview, I don't remember

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:34.259
which one had a sort of very interesting,
candid moment where he said, listen, you know,

00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:40.080
based on how long he's been doing this and
his age, am I going to get Medicare for all

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.080
passed?

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.080
No.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.580
But am I maybe going to continue doing things
that get more people health care?

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.759
Am I going to make incremental steps, sort
of, in a sense, channeling the idea of Ted

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:55.240
Kennedy, of incrementalism and taking steps
forward as you now are running for Senate?

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.900
And it's an interesting race, which we will
talk about.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.770
How do you balance the.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:03.009
Idea of what you would do if it were simply
up to you.

00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:08.759
And the reality of partizan gridlock needing
60 votes in the Senate to accomplish so many

00:05:08.759 --> 00:05:13.880
different things, the reality that the moneyed
rate is still extraordinarily effective at

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.580
using fundraising and lobbying to prevent
progress from being made.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.789
How do you balance what you would do if it
was just up to you and the reality of what

00:05:20.789 --> 00:05:23.289
can be accomplished in a six year term?

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:28.850
Speaker 2: Well, I also support Medicare for
All, but I'm a big believer that you make

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.180
progress where you can make progress.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:34.740
And, you know, at the end of the day, you
can't be progressive if you're not willing

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:35.949
to make progress.

00:05:35.949 --> 00:05:39.870
I'm enormously proud of what we did in the
Affordable Care Act, which brought health

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:43.250
care to tens of millions of people that didn't
have it otherwise.

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:49.449
I do think and I was a vocal advocate at the
time, it was a huge missed opportunity not

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:53.810
to pass the public option when we passed the
Affordable Care Act.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:59.509
That would have been, I think, the most substantial
downward driver of cost of anything we were

00:05:59.509 --> 00:06:05.640
doing at the time and would have forced not
only more reasonable costs for health care,

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:11.430
but that would have given us a model, a medicare
for all model to build on.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.389
So that was a terrible missed opportunity
nonetheless.

00:06:13.389 --> 00:06:17.110
But I have not passed the Affordable Care
Act because we didn't do that.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:22.530
Now, I'm glad I supported the final product,
even if it wasn't exactly what I wanted, because

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.710
it has done so much good.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:29.199
And I think we are moving inexorably towards
universal care.

00:06:29.199 --> 00:06:34.700
We just need to keep the fire lit, keep pushing
to get there quicker.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.449
And I'm still hopeful we will do it in Bernie
Sanders lifetime.

00:06:37.449 --> 00:06:39.599
A little on my own.

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:46.040
And but I don't think we let the enemy be
the or the good be the enemy of the the perfect

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:47.210
be the enemy of the good.

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:48.210
Excuse me.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:52.500
Speaker 1: When it comes to speaking of age,
you there's this interesting dynamic that's

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:56.740
developed over in the Republican presidential
primary where Nikki Haley announced that she's

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:57.740
running.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.880
And when she's been asked numerous times,
why would someone vote for you instead of

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:01.880
Trump?

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:05.260
Sometimes she's visibly confused, and when
she's able to put together an answer, it's

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:06.870
usually about identity.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.900
Despite claiming to be against identity politics,
she's put together.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:16.680
Well, I'm younger, I'm a woman, and I'm a
of a minority background of Indian background.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:22.129
In your Senate race, the retiring the outgoing
senator Dianne Feinstein, is around 90 years

00:07:22.129 --> 00:07:23.129
old.

00:07:23.129 --> 00:07:25.560
The you're running early sixties.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:26.690
Katie Porter's 49.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.759
Barbara Lee, 76 years old.

00:07:28.759 --> 00:07:35.550
How important to you do you think this idea
of Democrats needing a new generation in power,

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.220
how important do you think that is?

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:41.470
Speaker 2: You know, I think it really depends
entirely on the person.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:46.110
You know, so many young people are enthusiastic
supporters of Bernie Sanders, not because

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:52.540
of his age, but because a lot of his ideas
are new and his ideas appeal to young people.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:57.629
That, to me, I think, is more important than
what the age of the person is.

00:07:57.629 --> 00:07:59.199
That is, what are they advocating?

00:07:59.199 --> 00:08:01.810
What's their ability to get things done?

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:08.180
And so people often make that synonymous with
age, but it's not always the case.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:14.009
And I think, you know, Joe Biden has been
able to achieve great things for the country,

00:08:14.009 --> 00:08:19.009
including one of the most aggressive attacks
on climate change in our history.

00:08:19.009 --> 00:08:23.490
You might not expect that from a president
of his age, but nonetheless, you know, he

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:26.080
has been, I think, very effective and very
progressive.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.539
At the same time, voters are going to consider
these factors.

00:08:29.539 --> 00:08:32.830
Voters are going to look at age, they're going
to look at gender, they're going to look at

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:33.830
race.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:35.419
They should look at all of these things.

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:40.020
They're also going to look at who can deliver
for California, who is going to get things

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.260
done, who's going to improve the quality of
my life, who's going to help make sure I can

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:48.700
live in a in a good neighborhood with good
public schools that my kids will be able to

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:49.700
afford.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:50.900
Higher education won't be saddled with that.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.950
Who is going to make sure we can drink the
water and breathe the air?

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:58.950
At the end of the day, they're looking for
people who will deliver for California, and

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:01.750
I think they will look for that regardless
of age.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:05.760
I think they will look at who's got the track
record of getting things done.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:11.200
Speaker 1: When it comes to something like
tax policy to pick something extremely both

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.120
substantive and and concrete.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:18.310
There's the very general idea of the rich
should pay more.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:23.779
On the right, you have ideas from flat taxes
to cutting taxes for the rich, all sorts of

00:09:23.779 --> 00:09:25.470
different ideas.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:32.720
Right now we have a system where the highest
tax bracket federally, 37%, around half a

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:33.930
million dollars in income.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:34.930
I know that's not exact.

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:43.140
I don't remember if it's 460 or 520 this year
or exactly where it is in your ideal tax program.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:45.920
How much higher than 37 do you go, if at all?

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.760
And where would those higher tax brackets
start?

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:53.589
And one of the things I find is whenever I
ask elected officials this, rarely do I get

00:09:53.589 --> 00:09:55.380
numbers as answers.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:10:00.720
Speaker 2: Yeah, well, and I don't want to
disappoint you don't have a target number

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:01.720
either.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:02.720
Okay.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:08.519
But, you know, a big part of why I'm running
is to make the economy work for everyone in

00:10:08.519 --> 00:10:10.530
a way that is just not working right now.

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:13.029
And a big chunk of that is the tax code.

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:15.500
When I was a kid, my father was a salesman.

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:18.160
He made $18,000 a year.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:20.640
He bought a house for our family here.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:23.250
My mother, that cost $18,000.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:24.850
You can't do that now.

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.990
And not all of it is.

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:30.930
You know, the the as my Republican colleagues
would like to believe, the product of some

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:34.830
kind of impersonal market forces or Adam Smith's
invisible hand.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:38.940
A lot of the reason why the economy isn't
working is the product of a very invisible

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:43.889
hand of a very powerful lobbyist or a very
powerful corporate interest in terms of the

00:10:43.889 --> 00:10:50.149
tax code when we more than doubled, for example,
the child tax credit, yeah, that lifted 40%

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:52.190
of the kids out of poverty.

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:55.970
And when that was allowed to lapse, millions
fell back into poverty.

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:59.560
That was a policy choice we made, and it's
a terrible policy choice.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:04.000
So I would begin in terms of reforming the
tax code, more than doubling the child tax

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:10.610
credit, I would begin by providing very generous
tax subsidies for people trying to for child

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:14.470
care so we have accessible, affordable child
care.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:21.279
So more women in particular, but men, too,
can enter the job force because they can afford

00:11:21.279 --> 00:11:22.740
quality child care.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:28.910
I would do away with how we treat the carried
interest of hedge fund managers who get a

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:30.240
enormous tax break.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:31.889
They're some of the wealthiest people in the
country.

00:11:31.889 --> 00:11:37.670
Yeah, that break not because it makes sense,
it doesn't, but because they have very powerful

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:39.810
interests lobbying for them.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:44.279
So a lot of these changes would make the tax
code far more progressive.

00:11:44.279 --> 00:11:48.779
In addition to our we treat the top, top line
rate payers.

00:11:48.779 --> 00:11:52.610
Speaker 1: Would you like to see a top rate
that is higher than 37%?

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:59.350
Speaker 2: I'm certainly open to that so that
we have a tax code that is more progressive.

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:02.690
I just don't know that I can give you the
ideal number.

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:09.230
Yeah, but I think and going beyond the tax
code, there are innumerable ways that we can

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:15.769
make the economy work better for people where
we can incentivize the construction of more

00:12:15.769 --> 00:12:20.730
housing that's affordable, where we can ease
the, you know, the debt that students are

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:25.420
forced to live under, all of which I would
put under the category of making the economy

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:26.500
work for people again.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:30.380
Speaker 1: Let's talk about the cost of college
a little bit since you mentioned that.

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:32.399
So I'm trying to figure out.

00:12:32.399 --> 00:12:33.820
So my daughter is eight months old.

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:35.110
I started the 529.

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:36.110
Right.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:42.440
I'm pretending as though the world is normal
and it's going to be the same in 18 years

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.579
when it comes time for her to have the opportunity
to go to college.

00:12:45.579 --> 00:12:50.459
But I'm also doing the math, and I know that
for her to go to the same school that I or

00:12:50.459 --> 00:12:54.579
my girlfriend went to, if costs keep going
up at the same rate that they have been over

00:12:54.579 --> 00:13:01.810
the last 20 years, it'll be about $205,000
per year for tuition when she is 18.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:05.470
It sounds like that can't possibly happen
like that.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:09.560
Something has to break in the next 17 and
a half years or not, Right.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:15.480
I mean, if if student lending continues to
stay the way it is and people can just get

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.250
loans, I mean, it just the numbers don't seem
to make any sense.

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:21.089
Speaker 2: They don't make any sense.

00:13:21.089 --> 00:13:28.260
And it's a tragedy the way we've funded higher
education essentially on the back of students.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.050
I can relate a bit.

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:38.470
When I graduated from graduate school, my
loans were at nine, 12 and 14% interest and

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:42.320
my my loan payments were higher than my rent
for about the next ten years.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:43.320
Right.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:47.430
I went into the law where I could afford to
pay them off, which most people don't have

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:51.860
that luxury and it obviously circumscribed
what they could do with their careers, where

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:53.329
they can follow their passion.

00:13:53.329 --> 00:13:56.990
It's just a terrible way to fund education.

00:13:56.990 --> 00:14:02.089
And I do think we need a complete restructuring
so that we don't, you know, put on the backs

00:14:02.089 --> 00:14:07.940
of students the obligation to to pay for higher
education this way.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:09.670
It is unsustainable.

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:15.220
And and and sadly, this should have been done
years ago so that the people that have the

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:17.540
debt now didn't have to incur it.

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:22.769
And we cannot have a situation where your
kids are going to have to pay 200 K a year.

00:14:22.769 --> 00:14:27.500
That's just going to mean higher ed is only
available to the wealthiest Americans.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.570
So we need to change how we fund it.

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:32.940
We need to change the whole structure of it.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:37.560
We ought to, I think, begin by forgiving student
debt.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:42.209
And we certainly shouldn't be forcing students
to pay the government and.

00:14:42.209 --> 00:14:44.380
First on their student loans.

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:48.470
We shouldn't be the federal government shouldn't
be earning any kind of revenue or profit from

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:49.470
that.

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:53.210
Speaker 1: Couple other things in the in the
limited time we have from a political strategy

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:58.029
perspective, I'm curious, you know, there's
this discussion now about the Republican primary

00:14:58.029 --> 00:15:02.589
for 24 and what it will mean in a trickle
down sense, including for races like yours

00:15:02.589 --> 00:15:04.920
and in the House of Representatives and others.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:12.150
There is one feeling that if someone like
Ron DeSantis were to run, that he would be,

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:16.570
if he became the president, significantly
more dangerous than Donald Trump, and that

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:21.839
he is more committed, less distractible and
potentially more effective at doing really

00:15:21.839 --> 00:15:24.240
dangerous authoritarian things.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:28.600
And so in some sense, the country would be
better off with Donald Trump as the nominee

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:32.720
because it would he would not only be more
beatable if he was president, he would accomplish

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:34.410
fewer dangerous things.

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:39.910
And when it comes to races like yours, his
presence on the ticket may inspire more turnout

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.300
and help progressives and Democrats across
the country.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:48.819
Do you have a view on the strategic aspect
of what happens in that Republican primary?

00:15:48.819 --> 00:15:50.279
Speaker 2: I do.

00:15:50.279 --> 00:15:54.610
And I would put myself squarely, I guess,
in the opposite camp.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:16:00.430
I know many Democrats feel like they would
like, even aside from who became president,

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:03.880
they would like Trump as the nominee because
they believe he would be easier to beat.

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:08.019
Yes, he might be easier to beat than Ron DeSantis.

00:16:08.019 --> 00:16:16.720
But the danger of his presidency of a second
president to me is such a horrible prospect

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:19.500
that I don't even endure the possibility.

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:24.160
No, not that I have any say in who becomes
the Republican nominee, but I would rather

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:29.790
have the tougher person to beat than the even
the prospect that Donald Trump could be successful

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:31.170
in a general election.

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:38.779
I do think that he is, although he's not as
bright as the Santos, he is a uniquely talented

00:16:38.779 --> 00:16:39.779
grifter.

00:16:39.779 --> 00:16:40.779
Yes.

00:16:40.779 --> 00:16:44.670
Uniquely dangerous and shrewd divider of peoples.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:49.569
And so I think he would just tear the country
apart to Santos would be a different kind

00:16:49.569 --> 00:16:51.339
of horror show.

00:16:51.339 --> 00:16:56.459
But but to me, Trump learned from the first
four years.

00:16:56.459 --> 00:17:03.680
And, you know, he started out with a couple
of people of stature like Secretary Mattis

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:09.240
and consecutively got rid of anyone who had
any independent gravitas to stand up to him.

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:10.240
Right.

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:14.350
And and put in place increasingly sycophantic
people.

00:17:14.350 --> 00:17:18.970
He would start out with the worst of the worst
if he got another term.

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:25.000
And and one thing we did see with him is that
each abuse of presidential power was followed

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.970
by an even worse one, culminating in insurrection.

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:32.010
So to me, anything is preferable to that.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:36.080
Speaker 1: When he started saying, Your head
is like a watermelon.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.830
Were you hoping he would pick a different
fruit or vegetable?

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:41.390
Although watermelon is a great it's delicious.

00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:42.799
But what's your reaction to that?

00:17:42.799 --> 00:17:46.480
Speaker 2: Well, my reaction was, you know,
he went from calling me a pencil neck to a

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:47.820
watermelon head.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:51.240
And let me just say a watermelon head on a
pencil neck is a pretty tough balance.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:52.470
Speaker 1: As it is.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:59.110
Speaker 2: But my my predominant thought on
the on the various nicknames was, you know,

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:01.549
I thought he was better at it than that.

00:18:01.549 --> 00:18:06.190
The whole, you know, cardinal rule of nicknames
is you pick one and you stick to it.

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:07.190
Yes.

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:09.280
And he's had about eight of them for me over
the years.

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:15.620
And and so I don't think they've been as effective
as they could be if he just stuck to one.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:19.330
Speaker 1: It is interesting that he always
puts a caveat when he mentions you that you're

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:20.920
a bright guy.

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:21.920
I don't.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:24.830
Do you have any insight as to why he kind
of mentions that every single time?

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:27.570
Speaker 2: You know, it is it is interesting.

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:31.990
When I met him in the Oval Office during the
whole Devin Nunes midnight run thing.

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:36.900
Yeah, he came out from behind the Resolute
desk, where I have to say, he looked it looked

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:38.710
like the cover of Mad magazine to me.

00:18:38.710 --> 00:18:40.760
It definitely did not belong in that picture.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:42.409
He comes out from behind the desk.

00:18:42.409 --> 00:18:45.470
The first thing he does when he shakes my
hand and says, you know, you do a really good

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:46.700
job.

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:48.560
Presumably he means on TV.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:49.620
Oh, that.

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:51.470
That I understood.

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:56.070
Because that's what he that's what impresses
him how you do on TV.

00:18:56.070 --> 00:19:02.090
But I tell you, it was pretty awkward because
when somebody says something like that to

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:03.799
you, you know, you do a really good job.

00:19:03.799 --> 00:19:08.049
The first human impulses to want to say, well,
so do you.

00:19:08.049 --> 00:19:11.520
But there was no way I could bring myself
to saying that.

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:14.020
So it was quite an awkward pause after that
comment.

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:17.860
Speaker 1: That's a thank you, sir moment
as And it would become one of his sir anecdotes

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:19.280
that he's known for.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:25.179
Democratic Congressman Adam Schiff is running
in California for the Senate seat being vacated

00:19:25.179 --> 00:19:27.840
by longtime Senator Dianne Feinstein.

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:28.940
I know you're busy.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:31.260
I really appreciate your time and your insights
today.

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:32.260
Speaker 2: Great to be with you.

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:32.265
Thanks for having me on.

